fields:
- key: surface
  type: dropdown
  label: Surface
  description: 'Where you work with the run. Annotation (the default): you post messages to the run from the cell''s
    status pane — queued, or interrupting what it is doing — pointing at files and at elements inside them; it reads
    them, answers in place and edits the cell. Chat is a conversation. CLI is the harness''s own terminal: Claude
    Code''s, and Antigravity''s on the Google plan, is the vendor''s CLI on this machine (docker: none or full,
    commands ask or allow), signed in once per Key manager entry; every other harness runs its CLI in its container,
    under docker: full.'
  options:
  - value: annotation
    label: Annotation
  - value: chat
    label: Chat
  - value: terminal
    label: CLI
  default: annotation
  advanced: false
- key: session
  type: dropdown
  label: Session
  show_if:
    surface: annotation
  description: 'Interactive (the default) keeps the run listening for your messages until you stop it. One-shot works
    through the prompt — its first message — once, replies to it and ends when the work settles.'
  options:
  - value: oneshot
    label: One-shot
  - value: interactive
    label: Interactive
  default: interactive
  advanced: false
- key: backend
  type: dropdown
  label: Agent
  description: 'Harness from the selected model in Key Manager. Select a different model entry to change it. An
    Annotation run adopts the Docker grant its harness needs (docker: full for a harness container or the command
    sandbox), and its config and status row say so; on Chat and CLI the runner''s own Docker permission must place
    the harness, or the run stops before it starts. The CLI surface needs a harness with a TTY of its own, so
    pydantic-ai is not offered there.'
  account_role: model
  account_property: metadata.harness
  options:
  # <harness-catalog:backend-options>
  - value: pydantic-ai
    label: Pydantic AI
    description: 'docker: full sandboxes its commands in a container with the workspace mounted, and is recommended for commands: allow; under docker: none they run on this machine.'
    enable_if:
      not:
        surface: terminal
  - value: claude-code
    label: Claude Code
  - value: antigravity
    label: Antigravity
    description: 'With commands allow, permitted commands can read the provider key from the harness process environment. The CLI surface runs Google''s agy: on the Google plan it is this machine''s agy, which signs in through Google''s own browser flow once per Key manager entry and needs docker: none or full with commands ask or allow; on a Gemini key it runs in its container. The chat server''s conversation store is separate from the CLI''s: a chat session is not continued in the terminal. Cell MCP servers other than the Collimate bridge load lazily, behind a schema read the withheld view_file tool would do.'
  - value: codex
    label: Codex
  - value: goose
    label: goose
  - value: opencode
    label: opencode
  - value: qwen-code
    label: Qwen Code
  - value: cline
    label: Cline
    description: With commands allow, permitted commands can read the provider key from the harness process environment.
  - value: pi
    label: pi
  - value: hermes
    label: Hermes
  - value: vibe
    label: Mistral Vibe
    description: With commands allow, permitted commands can read the provider key from the harness process environment.
  - value: copilot
    label: GitHub Copilot CLI
    description: 'With commands allow, permitted commands run with the token or key stripped from their environment. Copilot loads a workspace''s hooks and MCP servers under --acp whether or not the folder is trusted, so no folder is trusted and the image''s root-owned managed settings lock hooks, MCP servers, custom agents and skills to plugins: files the agent writes there never run a command, at any commands level.'
  # </harness-catalog:backend-options>
  default: pydantic-ai
  advanced: false
- key: system_prompt
  type: textarea
  label: System prompt
  default: ''
  advanced: true
- key: mcp
  type: dropdown
  label: MCP servers
  show_if:
    surface: [annotation, chat]
  description: 'Attach MCP servers the runner declares in its _mcp config folder (transport http or stdio; env/headers
    must be ${account:role.secret_name} references — runner cells are exported/shared, literals would leak). off:
    never. http: remote/hosted servers only. http + stdio: also command servers — those run arbitrary code, so they
    need docker: full and a containerized CLI harness''s own container, never a host process; the engine rejects
    http_stdio on the host-locus harnesses (pydantic-ai, claude-code). Not available on cline / pi — their pinned
    ACP adapters accept but never wire session MCP servers (spec cell_mcp).'
  options:
  - 'off'
  - value: http
    enable_if:
      not:
        backend:
        # <harness-catalog:no-cell-mcp>
        - cline
        - pi
        # </harness-catalog:no-cell-mcp>
  - value: http_stdio
    label: http + stdio
    enable_if:
      not:
        backend:
        # <harness-catalog:no-cell-mcp>
        - cline
        - pi
        # </harness-catalog:no-cell-mcp>
  default: 'off'
  advanced: true
- key: approvals
  type: dropdown
  label: Approvals
  show_if:
    surface: [annotation, chat]
  description: 'How the run answers the harness''s permission requests other than shell commands. auto: answer from
    the runner grant — allow what it already permits, refuse the rest (the grant stays the security boundary). ask:
    surface each grant-permitted request as a card you answer in the run''s status pane (Annotation) or the chat
    (a request left unanswered for 10 minutes is refused). Shell commands follow the runner grant''s commands permission instead (deny / ask / allow — ask cards
    every command whatever this says; under allow the harness runs them without asking, so ask here cards only a
    command a harness still asks about). The in-process engine (pydantic-ai) has no permission flow of its own —
    only its commands, under commands: ask.'
  options:
  - auto
  - ask
  default: auto
  advanced: true
- key: selected_file_first
  type: checkbox
  label: Start from the selected file
  description: 'The file selected when the run starts becomes part of its first turn: a one-shot''s opening turn, the
    first batch of messages an interactive annotation run reads, the first chat message, or claude-code''s first TTY
    message (the one CLI that takes a first message). Off: the run ignores the selection.'
  show_if:
    any:
    - surface: [annotation, chat]
    - backend: claude-code
  default: false
  advanced: false
